course: dictionary
lesson: 1145
title: One word, many meanings: bridge
level: primary-5
stars: 2
explain: Many words have more than one meaning. Context, the words and sentences around a word, tells you which meaning the writer wants. «The bridge over the river was closed for repairs.» Here the words river and closed show that bridge means a structure that carries people across water.
explain: The same word can point to a completely different place. «The captain stood on the bridge of the ship and steered through the storm.» Ship and steered tell you that bridge means the part of a ship where the captain stands to steer it, not something you walk across.
explain: A bridge can also join things that are not made of stone or steel. «Trust is like a glass bridge between two people: one lie can crack it in a second.» Glass bridge is a comparison. The words between two people show that the bridge joins people, and that trust can break like glass.
fact: A bridge is something built over a river, road or valley so people or vehicles can go across.
fact: The bridge of your nose is the top part between your eyes. The bridge of a ship is where the captain steers it.
fact: To bridge a gap means to join two things together or to make a difference smaller.
fact: A beam bridge rests on supports, while an arch bridge pushes its weight sideways and down into the ground.
fact: Roman stone arch bridges still stand after about 2,000 years. Bridge is also a card game, so always check the context.
exercise: «A beam bridge rests on supports, while an arch pushes the weight sideways and down into the ground.» What is a bridge in this sentence? | The top part of your nose | *Something built over a river, road or valley | The place where a captain steers | A card game played with friends
exercise: «The captain stood on the bridge of the ship and steered through the storm.» What does bridge mean here? | A card game | The top part between your eyes | *The place on a ship where the captain steers
exercise: «Trust is like a glass bridge between two people.» What does the bridge join in this sentence? | Two cards in a game | A river and a road | A ship and a harbour | *Two people
test: «Roman stone arch bridges still stand after about 2,000 years.» Which kind of bridge pushes its weight sideways and down into the ground? | Glass | Beam | *Arch | Suspension
test: What does «to bridge a gap» mean? | To deal the cards for a game | To jump off a bridge into water | *To join two things or make a difference smaller | To paint the top of your nose
exam: In «Cross on the bridge, not on its railings, and never jump off a bridge into water», what is a bridge? | A card game | The place where a captain steers | The top part of your nose | *Something built over water so people can cross
